## One concept, one name

This tier is siege's "invariants," orchestration's "standing
decisions," and v4's policy tier, unified (v5 §4.5): a decision the
project holds itself to, first-class in the graph. A non-goal is
written the same way as any other policy — "we do not build X" is a
policy with negative content, not a different shape. If you're
drafting a decision not to do something, draft it as an ordinary
`<policy>`.

## What to produce

**`grade`** — a promote-from-prose ladder. Pick the grade this policy
is actually held to today, not the one you'd like it held to
eventually:

- `prose` — the default. Reconciliation's standing-decision check is
  the enforcement: every pass checks new content against approved
  policy prose. Always available, needs nothing built.
- `test` — the policy names specific tests that pin it, and those
  tests exist or are part of this work.
- `audit` — a registered check via the enforcement-profile mechanism
  catches violations mechanically.
- `runtime` — a guard in running code refuses the violation outright.

Grading `audit` or `runtime` with no real check behind it is
confidence without coverage — worse than an honest `prose`, because it
tells every scoped reader a guarantee holds that doesn't. If you're
not certain the artifact exists or is part of this work, grade
`prose`; the enforcement ticket the plane files on approval is where
raising the grade gets decided, not this draft.

**`mutability`** — `fixed` (automation- or correctness-load-bearing;
overriding is a load error), `tunable` (a declared parameter within a
fixed shape), or `optional` (default-on, replaceable with a declared
substitute). Strengthening a policy is always allowed; weakening is
only ever legal within the declared mutability.

**`<statement>`** — the decision itself, stated so a reader can check
a future change against it without reading this prompt again.

**`<applies_to_resp>` / `<applies_to_component>`** (both optional,
each a list of `<ref ref="...">` into the catalog above) — naming
neither makes this policy project-global, a real choice for policies
that bind the whole project, not a default for not having thought
about scope. Scope through `<applies_to_resp>` for almost everything:
a policy bound to responsibilities binds to *what the system does*,
and survives the system being re-decomposed later — components
inherit it through `fulfills`. Reach for `<applies_to_component>` only
when the policy is genuinely structural to one piece of implementation
and would still make sense stated about that component even if every
responsibility it fulfills moved elsewhere. When in doubt, scope
through resp.

**`<revisit_condition><condition>`** (required if this policy is a
deferral) — "never, argued" is a legal value, written out as the
argument for never revisiting; "not until X" must name a concrete X —
a milestone, a scale threshold, an external event — never "later" or
"when it becomes a priority." A deferral with no named condition is
exactly what this element exists to make impossible.

**`routing`** — `urgent` for compliance or structural guarantees the
automation or safety posture depends on; `feature` when the
enforcement gap should block the feature that introduced it; `debt`
(the default) otherwise. Route by what the policy actually protects,
not by what feels safest to write.
